================================================================================
Independent implementation and interoperability
================================================================================
OpenOSV lets owners of a DJI Osmo 360 read, stitch, colour and edit their own
.OSV recordings in software of their choice.  It is an independent
implementation, written in OpenOSV's own code.

What this repository and the binaries built from it do NOT contain:
  * DJI source code, or DJI binaries, libraries or other files from DJI
    software;
  * code transcribed from DJI software;
  * DJI neural-network models or weights;
  * DJI LUT files or LUT images, DJI masks or DJI remap tables;
  * DJI SDK headers, or DJI images or artwork.
The .cube files in luts/ contain no DJI data: each is generated in full by
`osvtool lut` from OpenOSV's own fitted models (see "Colour science
provenance" below).

What WAS done.  DJI's publicly distributed software was studied for
interoperability: to read the camera's files and to understand how DJI's own
tools render them.  The software studied was DJI Studio for Windows (version
1.0.0.24724), DJI's macOS Premiere reframe plug-in and DJI's macOS Premiere
importer, including its stitching library.  DJI's publicly distributed LUTs
were measured.  From that work OpenOSV took a limited set of numeric facts,
formulas and parameters, and implemented every one of them in its own code:
  * DJI's reframe camera model, its Zoom formula, zoom path, control limits
    and five presets;
  * the usable lens field of view (195.18 degrees);
  * the Dense Inverse Search optical-flow parameters and densification weight;
  * seam-finder cost weights and photometric rules;
  * the lens-protector field-angle correction (OpenOSV's own polynomial fit
    to DJI's curve; the curve itself is not included) and the order in which
    it is applied;
  * D-Log M curves, a camera primaries matrix and a Rec.709 look, fitted to
    measurements of DJI's LUTs (see below);
  * the meaning of the metadata's calibration slots and fields.
docs/LEGAL.md lists every item, where it lives in OpenOSV and where it came
from.  Nothing else from DJI software is used.

The .OSV container layout was determined from recorded files.  The per-frame
metadata's protobuf schema (message layout, field numbers, types and names)
and the lens calibration model were determined from recorded files and from
DJI's software (DJI Studio for Windows and DJI's macOS Premiere importer),
and the schema was cross-checked against the dvtm_oq101.proto
published by the open-source telemetry-parser project
(github.com/AdrianEddy/telemetry-parser, MIT OR Apache-2.0).
proto/dvtm_osmo360.proto is OpenOSV's own transcription of that schema.

================================================================================
Colour science provenance
================================================================================
* BT.2100 HLG / PQ transfer functions, BT.2020 and BT.709 primaries and the
  BT.2408 signal-level anchors are implemented from the public ITU-R
  Recommendations BT.2100-2, BT.2020-2 and BT.709-6 and the ITU-R Reports
  BT.2408-5 and BT.2390-8.  The HDR peak roll-off is the reference EETF of
  the public ITU-R Report BT.2408-7, Annex 5.
* The "DJI-matched" D-Log M decoding curve (kDlogMDjiRefit, `--fit dji`) is an
  analytic 7-parameter fit produced by scripts/fit_dlogm.py from 64
  neutral-axis measurements of DJI's publicly distributed D-Log M to HLG
  rendering (the FT_StyleGeneralDlogm2HLG style LUT bundled with DJI Studio).
  What is shipped is the fitted constants (include/osv/color/DlogM.h) and the
  64-value measurement table, recorded as the fit's provenance
  (scripts/fit_dlogm.py) and as the tests' reference
  (tests/unit/test_color.cpp); no DJI LUT data beyond those 64 values is
  included.
* The "Osmo 360" D-Log M decoding curve (kDlogMOsmo360, the default) is an
  analytic 7-parameter fit of the same form, produced by
  `scripts/fit_dlogm.py --from-cube DJI_Osmo360_DLogM_to_Rec709.cube` from the
  33 neutral-axis (R == G == B) samples of DJI's publicly distributed Osmo 360
  D-Log M to Rec.709 LUT (as bundled with DJI Studio).  Measuring a transfer
  function is what was done, not copying a table: what is shipped is the
  seven fitted constants (include/osv/color/DlogM.h) and the 33 measured
  diagonal values, recorded as the tests' reference
  (tests/unit/DjiReference.h).  The fit scripts read the LUT from a local
  copy (DJI Studio installs one) and print only fitted constants.
* The Osmo 360 native primaries matrix (kNativeToRec2020_Osmo360, the
  default) is OpenOSV's least-squares fit, produced by
  `scripts/fit_primaries.py --from-cube DJI_Osmo360_DLogM_to_Rec709.cube`
  from measurements of all 35937 entries of the same LUT.  Only the nine
  fitted constants are shipped (include/osv/color/Matrices.h).
* The "Pocket 3" D-Log M curve (kDlogMPocket3) reproduces the numeric
  constants of the publicly published community fit by Thatcher Freeman
  ("DJI Pocket 3 D-Log M to DWG.dctl", github.com/thatcherfreeman/
  dwg-transforms), and kNativeToRec2020_Pocket3 is that file's colour-chart
  matrix (camera native to DaVinci Wide Gamut) composed with the DaVinci Wide
  Gamut to Rec.2020 conversion.  The constants are facts about a transfer
  function and a colour matrix; no source code from that repository is
  included.
* The "DJI Studio" Rec.709 look (the default Rec.709 rendering) is OpenOSV's own
  parametric model - a 3x3, a 17-knot monotone tone curve, a highlight hue
  blend, a second 3x3 and a soft gamut compression, 46 stored constants of
  which 38 are free parameters
  (include/osv/color/Look.h) - fitted by scripts/fit_look.py to measurements of
  DJI's publicly distributed Osmo 360 D-Log M to Rec.709 LUT (as bundled with
  DJI Studio).  What is shipped is the fitted constants and 72 measured entries
  recorded as the tests' reference (tests/unit/DjiReference.h, which also holds
  the 33 neutral-axis values above: 105 of the LUT's 35937 entries in all); no
  other DJI LUT data is redistributed, and the Rec.709 .cube in luts/ is
  generated by `osvtool lut` from the fitted model.  The gamut compression
  uses the published curve of the ACES Reference Gamut Compression (Academy
  of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences), implemented from its mathematical
  description; no ACES code is included.

